The current `dots.cpp` implementation runs **normal DOTS**, not DOTS-CASCADE.

The key call is:

```118:125:/Users/sinchunyeung/Simplification-of-Trajectory-Streams/traj-compression/online/DOTS/dots.cpp
QVector<int> simplifiedIndex;

qDebug() << "Running DOTS simplification (Threshold:" << lssdThreshold << ")...";
auto _core_start = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
DotsSimplifier::batchDotsByIndex(x, y, t, simplifiedIndex, lssdThreshold);
auto _core_end = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
```

`batchDotsByIndex(...)` creates one `DotsSimplifier` instance and feeds the trajectory into it:

```141:169:/Users/sinchunyeung/Simplification-of-Trajectory-Streams/traj-compression/online/DOTS/DotsSimplifier.cpp
void DotsSimplifier::batchDotsByIndex(const QVector<double> &x, const QVector<double> &y, const QVector<double> &t,
                                      QVector<int> &simplifiedIndex, double lssdThreshold)
{
    DotsSimplifier simplifier;
    simplifier.setParameters(lssdThreshold);
    int pointCount = x.count();

    for (int i = 0; i < pointCount; ++i) {
        simplifier.feedData(x.at(i), y.at(i), t.at(i));
        // ...
    }

    simplifier.finish();
}
```

DOTS-CASCADE would use:

```cpp
DotsSimplifier::batchDotsCascadeByIndex(
    x, y, t, simplifiedIndex, lssdThreshold
);
```

That function exists in the implementation, but `dots.cpp` does not call it.

So the current structure is:

- `dots.cpp`: command-line driver
- `DotsSimplifier::batchDotsByIndex`: normal DOTS
- `DotsSimplifier::batchDotsCascadeByIndex`: DOTS-CASCADE, currently unused by this executable
- `simplify_with_gui.cpp`: separate GUI application, unrelated to whether normal DOTS or cascade is used

The paper describes normal DOTS as the optimization-based algorithm with approximately \(O(N^2/M)\) complexity, while DOTS-CASCADE chains multiple DOTS modules to achieve approximately \(O(N)\) time at the cost of potentially more greedy simplification.

